{"author":"Rebar Bot Nava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Observed defect\nThree open comment-sweep leaves — improbable-crimson-hare, nonfat-pernicious-aracari, and orthogonal-shrewd-taruca — carry saved PASS plan-review SSHSIG envelopes signed by the original nava-opcert-prod key. Their claims fail `signature: mismatch; ssh-keygen -Y verify rejected signature (exit 255)`. All 3 remain open and unclaimed.\n\nThe envelopes embed the original public key `ssh-ed25519 AAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAIB6u+ddQiB4ck/ToORQkQwtehODF4VqLirMIUNFpobZZ`, which is still an unrevoked pin in `.rebar/trusted_environments.yaml`. The same pin file also carries the rotated key `ssh-ed25519 AAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAIGA6u+J56OuLaR8xsCqyIFZisj/Pke3dkNIfgifykhfi`; a recent certified ticket uses it. An isolated SSHSIG verification of improbable-crimson-hare's unchanged envelope returns `certified` under the historical pin and `mismatch` / exit 255 under the rotated pin.\n\n`src/rebar/_opcert_verify.py::_opcert_trust_root` returns only `_opcert_own_public_keys(tracker)` when the certificate principal equals the current principal. That skips the historical pinned keyring for the same principal. The verifier therefore rejects a valid pre-rotation certificate.\n\nAdjacent finding: MCP `plan_review_status` also returns an output-validation error for these tickets because its declared verdict enum omits `mismatch`; the underlying claim error still exposes the mismatch.\n\n## Decision (operator, 2026-10-24)\nOption 1: for the certificate's own principal, trust this environment's current key plus every pinned key for that principal in `.rebar/trusted_environments.yaml` that is not revoked, with no era check. The operator chose this over threading the certificate's tickets-log position into `verify_opcert_record` so key eras apply.\n\n## Verification (2026-10-24, current main)\n- `_opcert_trust_root` (`src/rebar/_opcert_verify.py`, complexity 5; `verify_opcert_record` 8 against a ceiling of 15) returns only `_opcert_own_public_keys(tracker)` when the principal equals the own principal and an own key exists; it never reads the pinned keyring on that path.\n- `_pinned_environment_keys` returns every keyring entry with a `public_key`, including entries with a non-null `revoked_at_log_position`.\n- The signature verdict reaches `plan_review_status` unchanged (`claim_gate_check` returns `result.get(\"verdict\")` from `signing.verify_signature`). The verdicts that path can emit besides `certified` and `unsigned` are `mismatch` (`attest/opcert.py`, `attest/sshsig.py`), `invalid` (`attest/sshsig.py`, `_opcert_verify.py`), `foreign_key` (`_opcert_verify.py`), `unavailable` (`attest/sshsig.py`, when `ssh-keygen` cannot run), and `unknown_kind` / `unknown_scheme` (`attest/registry.py`). `src/rebar/schemas/plan_review_status.schema.json` declares an enum without any of those 6, so a non-certified signature verdict fails output validation. This confirms the ticket's adjacent finding and widens it from `mismatch` to the whole reachable set. Three verdicts are deliberately NOT added because they are unreachable here: `unknown_principal`, `subject_mismatch` and `malformed_statement` come only from the authorship path (`attest/authorship.py`), and `key_not_valid_at_era` only from `verify_required_environment`, which the `verify-opcert` merge-gate command calls.\n- Not reproduced end to end here: that needs an envelope signed under the pre-rotation key.\n\n## Approach\n1. Keep today's own-key verification first, unchanged. Only when the principal is the own principal, an own key exists, and that verification returns `mismatch`, retry the same envelope under the pinned keys for that principal whose `revoked_at_log_position` is null and that are not already own keys. If the retry certifies, report `trust_basis` `pinned_environment`; otherwise return the original `mismatch`. The retry lives in a small helper so `verify_opcert_record` stays well under its complexity ceiling. `trust_basis` is a typed literal (`own_key`, `pinned_environment`, `envelope_key`, `None` in `src/rebar/types.py`), so no new label is introduced, and a certificate under the current own key still reports `own_key`.\n2. Give `_pinned_environment_keys` an `unrevoked_only` parameter. It is used on BOTH own-principal paths: the retry in step 1, and the existing fall-through taken when the principal is the own principal but no own key is held in this clone (`_opcert_trust_root` currently returns every pinned key there, revoked ones included). After this change a revoked key is never trusted for the own principal on either path. The foreign-principal path keeps today's revoked-inclusive behaviour; that path trusting revoked keys is a separate defect, filed as its own bug (`0ed1-128f-b9a5-4c39`).\n3. Add the 6 reachable signature verdicts above to the `verdict` enum of `src/rebar/schemas/plan_review_status.schema.json`, and update the verdict lists in the `plan_review_status` docstrings (`src/rebar/_mcp_reads.py`, `src/rebar/llm/plan_review/attest_gate.py`).\n\n## Scope\nIn scope: `src/rebar/_opcert_verify.py`, `src/rebar/schemas/plan_review_status.schema.json`, the two docstrings and the `tests/interfaces/facades/mcp_tool_surface_golden.json` entry that embeds the `plan_review_status` docstring verbatim (regenerated or edited to match, and checked by the test that reads it), and tests. Out of scope: era validity of pinned keys, the foreign-principal revocation behaviour, and re-signing the 3 comment-sweep leaves.\n\n## Testing\n```\npytest tests/unit/test_opcert_local_verify_binding.py -q\nmake verify\n```\nNew cases, built with two real keys under one principal and a pin file listing both: a certificate signed by the old pinned key verifies after the own key changes to the new one, with `trust_basis` `pinned_environment`; a certificate signed by a key that is not pinned still returns `mismatch`; a certificate signed by a pinned key marked revoked returns `mismatch`, both when an own key exists and when none is held in the clone (the fall-through path); a certificate with a changed payload still fails; a certificate under the current own key still verifies with `trust_basis` `own_key`. The real `mismatch` result (an envelope verified under a key it was not signed with) is produced through `verify_opcert_record` and validated against the schema; a schema test also validates a result carrying each of the other 5 reachable verdicts, and checks the 3 unreachable ones are absent from the enum. Failure case: the old-key case fails on main with `mismatch`, and the schema case fails on main for `mismatch`.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] A certificate signed by a pinned, unrevoked historical key of the own principal verifies after the own key has rotated (checked: new test, which fails on main).\n- [ ] A certificate signed by an unpinned key, by a pinned key marked revoked, or with a changed payload or signature still fails (checked: new tests).\n- [ ] `plan_review_status` output validates against its schema for each signature verdict, so a mismatch is returned as a structured verdict (checked: new schema test, which fails on main for `mismatch`).\n- [ ] The era-validity limit of this fix is documented where the own-principal trust root is chosen (checked: comment in the diff).\n- [ ] `make verify` is green on the Linux CI gate (`Verified +1` on the Gerrit change).\n\n## Follow-up\nThe foreign-principal path trusting revoked pinned keys, and era validity for pinned keys, are tracked in their own bug.\n"}},"env_id":"492a5c71-6cda-48a6-8134-02ff6a672739","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1792888366591743001,"uuid":"1181fce1-2242-4b6f-a890-92d83ea4beec"}